1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,160
On the morning of July the
twenty-fifth, 1884, a thirteen-foot boat

2
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:10,140
was drifting in the South Atlantic,
about sixteen hundred miles from

3
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,250
the Cape of Good Hope.

4
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:13,130
There were four men in it.

5
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:15,050
They had been there for twenty days.

6
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:19,790
When their yacht went down, they
had two tins of turnips.

7
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:22,330
On the fourth day they
caught a small turtle.

8
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,790
For the last eight days, they
had eaten nothing at all.

9
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:29,940
The youngest was the cabin
boy, Richard Parker.

10
00:00:30,370 --> 00:00:34,330
He was seventeen. He had been
drinking seawater, and he was lying

11
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:37,040
in the bottom of the boat,
too weak to move.

12
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:44,700
That morning, the captain, Tom Dudley,
said a prayer, asking forgiveness

13
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:49,740
for them all. Then he told the boy that
his time had come, and killed

14
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:50,850
him with a knife.

15
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:56,870
Four days later, a passing ship
found three men alive.

16
00:00:57,220 --> 00:00:58,910
They had lived on the boy's body.

17
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:05,470
When they reached England, they told
the authorities exactly what

18
00:01:05,490 --> 00:01:09,640
they had done. They believed the law
of the sea was on their side.

19
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:13,360
Three months later, two of them were
sentenced to death for murder.

20
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:15,380
They served six months.

21
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:22,180
So, was it murder? If four people are
going to die, and killing one

22
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:25,190
would save the other three,
is that a crime?

23
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:29,660
And if the law says it is, who gets to
decide whose life counts for

24
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:30,070
less?

25
00:01:32,410 --> 00:01:35,430
We went through the court's own record
of what happened in that boat,

26
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:39,570
the judgment that still binds English
courts, and the cases that have

27
00:01:39,590 --> 00:01:43,540
tested it since, from conjoined
twins to hijacked planes.

28
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:47,900
At the end, we'll give you a verdict,
and tell you how sure we are.

29
00:01:59,805 --> 00:02:02,605
The Mignonette was a fifty-two-foot
yacht built for cruising near

30
00:02:02,625 --> 00:02:04,285
the coast, not for crossing oceans.

31
00:02:04,305 --> 00:02:08,445
A lawyer in Sydney had bought her in
England and needed her sailed

32
00:02:08,465 --> 00:02:14,155
out to Australia. Tom Dudley,
thirty-one, took the job, with a mate,

33
00:02:14,685 --> 00:02:19,995
Edwin Stephens, a sailor, Edmund
Brooks, and a cabin boy.

34
00:02:20,685 --> 00:02:24,735
They left Southampton on May
the nineteenth, 1884.

35
00:02:26,155 --> 00:02:31,095
On July the fifth, in a storm far out
in the South Atlantic, a wave

36
00:02:31,115 --> 00:02:32,725
smashed in the yacht's side.

37
00:02:33,435 --> 00:02:35,405
She sank in about five minutes.

38
00:02:35,955 --> 00:02:39,815
The four men got into the dinghy:
thirteen feet long, with planks

39
00:02:39,835 --> 00:02:41,305
about a quarter of an inch thick.

40
00:02:43,205 --> 00:02:46,585
Almost everything we know for certain
about the next three weeks comes

41
00:02:46,605 --> 00:02:50,965
from one document. At their trial,
the jury didn't decide whether

42
00:02:50,985 --> 00:02:55,105
they were guilty. It wrote down the
facts and left the question of

43
00:02:55,125 --> 00:02:56,375
guilt to the judges.

44
00:02:57,445 --> 00:03:01,415
That's called a special verdict, and
it's the record we'll quote.

45
00:03:02,935 --> 00:03:07,735
It says they had no water, and no food
except two one-pound tins of

46
00:03:07,755 --> 00:03:11,285
turnips. The turtle lasted
until the twelfth day.

47
00:03:12,255 --> 00:03:13,545
After that, nothing.

48
00:03:14,135 --> 00:03:17,765
When it rained, they caught the
water in their oilskin capes.

49
00:03:18,465 --> 00:03:23,685
By the eighteenth day, they had gone
seven days without food and five

50
00:03:23,705 --> 00:03:27,795
without water. Richard Parker had
been drinking seawater.

51
00:03:28,365 --> 00:03:30,275
He was the weakest of them by far.

52
00:03:32,275 --> 00:03:35,775
On the twenty-fourth, Dudley proposed
that they draw lots to decide

53
00:03:35,795 --> 00:03:38,785
who should die so the
others could live.

54
00:03:39,595 --> 00:03:42,325
Brooks refused. The boy
was never asked.

55
00:03:42,795 --> 00:03:43,865
No lots were drawn.

56
00:03:45,565 --> 00:03:48,915
That evening, the record says, they
spoke of their families.

57
00:03:49,445 --> 00:03:53,285
Dudley said that if no ship was in
sight by morning, the boy should

58
00:03:53,305 --> 00:03:56,575
be killed. In the morning,
there was no ship.

59
00:03:57,425 --> 00:03:59,915
Stephens agreed. Brooks did not.

60
00:04:01,635 --> 00:04:04,915
The record says the boy was lying in
the bottom of the boat, quite

61
00:04:04,935 --> 00:04:06,985
helpless, unable to resist.

62
00:04:07,415 --> 00:04:10,365
And it says he never agreed
to his being killed.

63
00:04:12,965 --> 00:04:17,675
Four days later, the German ship
Moctezuma, bound for Hamburg, picked

64
00:04:18,065 --> 00:04:20,425
them up. So, the first
weight on the scale.

65
00:04:20,445 --> 00:04:24,625
The jury found that without the boy's
body, the men would probably

66
00:04:24,645 --> 00:04:27,115
have died within those four days.

67
00:04:27,585 --> 00:04:30,155
It found that he would probably
have died before them.

68
00:04:30,585 --> 00:04:34,285
And it found one more thing: there was
no greater necessity for killing

69
00:04:34,305 --> 00:04:36,155
him than any of the other three.

70
00:04:42,284 --> 00:04:46,424
On September the sixth, they landed at
Falmouth in Cornwall, and they

71
00:04:46,444 --> 00:04:48,354
did something that
startled the officials.

72
00:04:49,064 --> 00:04:50,594
They told them everything.

73
00:04:51,664 --> 00:04:54,574
Dudley even asked for his knife
back, as a keepsake.

74
00:04:55,324 --> 00:04:57,394
Their dinghy was put on
show in the town.

75
00:04:58,854 --> 00:05:01,964
They talked because they believed
they had done nothing wrong.

76
00:05:02,954 --> 00:05:04,284
Sailors had a rule for this.

77
00:05:04,514 --> 00:05:07,604
When a crew was starving in an
open boat, they drew lots.

78
00:05:08,154 --> 00:05:10,504
The loser died so the
others could live.

79
00:05:11,134 --> 00:05:13,124
It was called the custom of the sea.

80
00:05:14,504 --> 00:05:15,714
It wasn't a legend.

81
00:05:16,484 --> 00:05:19,834
In 1820, a whale sank the
Nantucket ship Essex.

82
00:05:20,484 --> 00:05:24,274
Months later, in one of its open
boats, the survivors drew lots.

83
00:05:24,884 --> 00:05:27,594
It fell on a young
sailor, Owen Coffin.

84
00:05:28,144 --> 00:05:30,434
He was shot, and the others ate him.

85
00:05:31,124 --> 00:05:33,774
The first mate published the
account the following year.

86
00:05:35,074 --> 00:05:37,444
An American court had looked
at the custom once.

87
00:05:37,854 --> 00:05:42,934
In 1841, after the ship William Brown
hit ice, sailors threw fourteen

88
00:05:42,954 --> 00:05:45,244
passengers out of an
overloaded lifeboat.

89
00:05:45,894 --> 00:05:49,684
One of them, a sailor named Holmes,
was convicted of manslaughter.

90
00:05:50,454 --> 00:05:53,384
The judge's objection wasn't that
anyone was sacrificed.

91
00:05:53,814 --> 00:05:55,184
It was how they were chosen.

92
00:05:55,934 --> 00:06:00,494
When a sacrifice is necessary, he said,
the selection is by lot: the

93
00:06:00,514 --> 00:06:04,444
fairest way, and in some
sort, an appeal to God.

94
00:06:05,824 --> 00:06:08,914
In the Mignonette, there was
no appeal to anyone.

95
00:06:09,404 --> 00:06:10,354
There were no lots.

96
00:06:11,144 --> 00:06:12,414
The boy wasn't asked.

97
00:06:12,844 --> 00:06:17,024
By the sailors' own custom, he was owed
a fair chance, and he didn't

98
00:06:17,044 --> 00:06:17,594
get one.

99
00:06:19,714 --> 00:06:22,784
Edmund Brooks is the hardest part
of the story to pin down.

100
00:06:23,234 --> 00:06:24,884
The record says he refused.

101
00:06:25,454 --> 00:06:26,644
He ate with the others.

102
00:06:27,174 --> 00:06:31,014
The Crown dropped the case against him,
and he gave evidence against

103
00:06:31,034 --> 00:06:35,424
his shipmates. Dudley always said
that all three had agreed.

104
00:06:37,444 --> 00:06:39,094
Falmouth took the sailors' side.

105
00:06:39,784 --> 00:06:41,334
Townspeople put up their bail.

106
00:06:41,724 --> 00:06:46,384
And Richard Parker's older brother,
Daniel, himself a seaman, came

107
00:06:46,404 --> 00:06:48,474
to a hearing and shook their hands.

108
00:06:49,974 --> 00:06:51,544
The government saw it differently.

109
00:06:53,074 --> 00:06:56,434
Survivors had told stories like this
before, and nobody in England

110
00:06:56,454 --> 00:06:58,344
had ever been convicted for it.

111
00:06:58,834 --> 00:07:03,184
The Home Secretary, Sir William
Harcourt, decided to prosecute.

112
00:07:03,994 --> 00:07:07,514
This time, a court would settle whether
hunger could ever be a defence

113
00:07:07,534 --> 00:07:08,244
to murder.

114
00:07:09,824 --> 00:07:12,914
One detail sounds invented, and isn't.

115
00:07:13,704 --> 00:07:17,264
Forty-six years earlier, Edgar Allan Poe
wrote a novel in which shipwrecked

116
00:07:17,284 --> 00:07:21,914
men draw lots, and the man who
loses is killed and eaten.

117
00:07:22,604 --> 00:07:24,074
His name is Richard Parker.

118
00:07:24,824 --> 00:07:28,444
If it sounds familiar, it's also the
name of the tiger in Life of

119
00:07:28,464 --> 00:07:28,894
Pi.

120
00:07:30,754 --> 00:07:32,184
So, the second weight.

121
00:07:32,814 --> 00:07:36,844
A custom that sailors believed in,
and a town that forgave them.

122
00:07:37,454 --> 00:07:41,564
But the custom asked for a fair
lottery, and the boy never got one.

123
00:07:47,465 --> 00:07:50,645
The trial opened at Exeter on
November the sixth.

124
00:07:50,665 --> 00:07:54,445
The defence, paid for by public
subscription, argued that the weakest

125
00:07:54,465 --> 00:07:55,735
must go to the wall.

126
00:07:56,265 --> 00:07:59,465
The judge, Baron Huddleston, made
sure the decision wouldn't rest

127
00:07:59,485 --> 00:08:00,795
with a sympathetic jury.

128
00:08:01,385 --> 00:08:05,205
He had them return a special verdict, a
device that hadn't been used

129
00:08:05,225 --> 00:08:09,795
in about a century, and sent the
question to five judges in London.

130
00:08:11,375 --> 00:08:15,795
On December the ninth, the Lord Chief
Justice, Lord Coleridge, gave

131
00:08:15,815 --> 00:08:18,025
their answer. It was unanimous.

132
00:08:18,875 --> 00:08:20,425
The killing was wilful murder.

133
00:08:21,845 --> 00:08:24,535
His reasoning still gets
quoted in courts today.

134
00:08:25,425 --> 00:08:28,595
To preserve one's life, he
said, is generally a duty.

135
00:08:29,165 --> 00:08:32,955
But it may be the plainest and the
highest duty to sacrifice it.

136
00:08:33,605 --> 00:08:37,735
Soldiers do it. Sailors are expected
to do it for their passengers.

137
00:08:39,295 --> 00:08:42,125
Then he asked the question at
the centre of this case.

138
00:08:42,815 --> 00:08:45,305
Who is to be the judge of
this sort of necessity?

139
00:08:45,795 --> 00:08:49,985
By what measure is the value of one life
against another to be measured?

140
00:08:50,695 --> 00:08:54,125
Is it to be strength, or
intellect, or what?

141
00:08:56,465 --> 00:08:57,675
His point was simple.

142
00:08:58,245 --> 00:09:02,125
A necessity defence lets the person who
gains from the killing decide

143
00:09:02,145 --> 00:09:03,355
that it was necessary.

144
00:09:03,845 --> 00:09:09,415
And in this case, he said, the weakest,
the youngest, the most unresisting,

145
00:09:09,545 --> 00:09:10,275
was chosen.

146
00:09:11,835 --> 00:09:15,145
And then he admitted something
judges rarely say out loud.

147
00:09:15,715 --> 00:09:19,955
We are often compelled, he wrote, to
set up standards we cannot reach

148
00:09:19,975 --> 00:09:24,265
ourselves, and to lay down rules which
we could not ourselves satisfy.

149
00:09:26,785 --> 00:09:28,295
He sentenced them to death.

150
00:09:28,925 --> 00:09:32,945
The jury had recommended mercy, and
within days the sentence was commuted

151
00:09:32,965 --> 00:09:34,315
to six months in prison.

152
00:09:35,125 --> 00:09:38,455
The government's own law officers thought
that if it had been manslaughter,

153
00:09:38,925 --> 00:09:40,875
no judge would have given
more than three.

154
00:09:42,255 --> 00:09:45,205
They were released in the
spring of 1885.

155
00:09:45,735 --> 00:09:50,105
Dudley moved to Sydney, and died there
in a plague outbreak in 1900.

156
00:09:51,195 --> 00:09:53,825
He never accepted that what
he did was wrong.

157
00:09:54,575 --> 00:09:58,015
In Southampton, Richard Parker's
memorial carries a line from the

158
00:09:58,035 --> 00:10:01,625
Bible. Lord, lay not this
sin to their charge.

159
00:10:04,425 --> 00:10:05,735
So, the third weight.

160
00:10:06,285 --> 00:10:09,725
The court said nobody may decide that
another person's life is worth

161
00:10:09,745 --> 00:10:12,295
less than their own, and
call it necessity.

162
00:10:13,065 --> 00:10:14,835
Then the state showed mercy anyway.

163
00:10:21,006 --> 00:10:22,716
The rule still stands in England.

164
00:10:23,026 --> 00:10:27,146
In 1987, the House of Lords held that
even a threat to your own life

165
00:10:27,166 --> 00:10:30,916
is no defence to murder, and
relied on this case to do it.

166
00:10:31,936 --> 00:10:35,626
But in 2000, the rule met a
case it couldn't hold.

167
00:10:36,456 --> 00:10:38,426
Jodie and Mary were conjoined twins.

168
00:10:39,216 --> 00:10:40,726
Mary could not live on her own.

169
00:10:41,876 --> 00:10:46,186
Doctors said that, left joined, both
would probably die within months.

170
00:10:46,976 --> 00:10:49,766
Separating them would save
Jodie and kill Mary.

171
00:10:50,136 --> 00:10:52,686
The Court of Appeal said the
operation was lawful.

172
00:10:54,066 --> 00:10:58,096
One judge, Lord Justice Brooke, went
straight back to the Mignonette.

173
00:10:58,826 --> 00:11:01,916
Coleridge had asked who
decides, and by what measure.

174
00:11:02,546 --> 00:11:05,396
Here, Brooke said, nobody chose Mary.

175
00:11:06,186 --> 00:11:09,156
Her condition did. He set
out three tests.

176
00:11:09,626 --> 00:11:13,856
The act must be needed to avoid an evil
that is inevitable and irreparable.

177
00:11:14,306 --> 00:11:16,396
No more must be done
than is necessary.

178
00:11:16,666 --> 00:11:20,356
And the harm done must not be out of
proportion to the harm avoided.

179
00:11:22,356 --> 00:11:26,786
He gave another example, from the
Zeebrugge ferry disaster in 1987.

180
00:11:27,696 --> 00:11:30,456
Passengers were climbing a rope ladder
out of the water when a young

181
00:11:30,476 --> 00:11:33,766
man froze on it, blocking the way
for at least ten minutes.

182
00:11:34,276 --> 00:11:37,406
An army corporal had him pushed off
so the others could climb.

183
00:11:37,876 --> 00:11:39,186
He was never seen again.

184
00:11:39,856 --> 00:11:41,126
No one was prosecuted.

185
00:11:41,696 --> 00:11:44,656
A leading criminal lawyer thought a
court could tell this apart from

186
00:11:44,676 --> 00:11:49,126
the Mignonette, because the young man
had, in effect, chosen himself.

187
00:11:51,066 --> 00:11:53,236
Germany faced the modern version.

188
00:11:54,086 --> 00:11:57,406
After September the eleventh, it
passed a law letting the military

189
00:11:57,426 --> 00:11:59,576
shoot down a hijacked passenger plane.

190
00:12:00,026 --> 00:12:03,596
In 2006, its constitutional
court struck it down.

191
00:12:04,246 --> 00:12:07,536
Killing the innocent passengers to save
people on the ground, it said,

192
00:12:07,766 --> 00:12:09,576
would treat them as mere objects.

193
00:12:10,206 --> 00:12:13,286
Shooting down a plane carrying only
the attackers would be another

194
00:12:13,306 --> 00:12:13,856
matter.

195
00:12:15,536 --> 00:12:17,976
America's Model Penal Code
went the other way.

196
00:12:17,996 --> 00:12:22,616
Its choice of evils rule says an act
can be justified if it avoids

197
00:12:22,636 --> 00:12:26,406
a greater harm, and it
doesn't exclude killing.

198
00:12:27,096 --> 00:12:30,276
Hawaii's official commentary on its
version says it could justify

199
00:12:30,296 --> 00:12:33,726
taking a life to avoid a far
greater loss of life.

200
00:12:35,406 --> 00:12:38,356
Philosophers have kept rebuilding
the Mignonette ever since.

201
00:12:38,966 --> 00:12:44,086
In 1949, the law professor Lon Fuller
invented a case: five explorers

202
00:12:44,106 --> 00:12:47,406
trapped in a cave kill one of their
own after a throw of the dice

203
00:12:47,426 --> 00:12:50,276
he had proposed, then
tried to back out of.

204
00:12:50,806 --> 00:12:54,226
His fictional judges split, and law
students have been splitting over

205
00:12:54,246 --> 00:12:55,996
it for more than seventy years.

206
00:12:56,566 --> 00:13:01,326
The trolley problem, invented in 1967,
is the same question with the

207
00:13:01,346 --> 00:13:02,416
hunger taken out.

208
00:13:04,196 --> 00:13:05,586
So, the fourth weight.

209
00:13:05,936 --> 00:13:09,006
The law has bent, but only
in one direction.

210
00:13:09,696 --> 00:13:14,196
Where circumstances pick out who dies,
the twins, the frozen man on

211
00:13:14,216 --> 00:13:16,496
the ladder, courts have found a way.

212
00:13:16,516 --> 00:13:22,316
Where people pick, by weakness or by
usefulness, the answer is still

213
00:13:22,336 --> 00:13:22,826
no.

214
00:13:28,897 --> 00:13:31,447
First, the strongest case for the men.

215
00:13:32,197 --> 00:13:33,887
Four people were going to die.

216
00:13:34,397 --> 00:13:38,707
Three lived. The boy was dying
first, and the jury said so.

217
00:13:39,357 --> 00:13:43,377
Nobody chose to be in that boat, and no
law written on land was ever

218
00:13:43,397 --> 00:13:47,737
meant for it. Anyone who says they'd
have done differently has never

219
00:13:47,757 --> 00:13:48,447
been there.

220
00:13:49,647 --> 00:13:51,127
Now the strongest case against them.

221
00:13:51,147 --> 00:13:59,827
They chose the weakest and youngest
person in the boat, the one who

222
00:13:59,847 --> 00:14:01,127
couldn't object, and didn't ask him.

223
00:14:01,147 --> 00:14:03,447
They didn't even follow their
own custom's lottery.

224
00:14:03,467 --> 00:14:06,907
The jury found there was no greater
necessity to kill him than any

225
00:14:06,927 --> 00:14:10,587
of them. If that counts as necessity,
then whoever is strongest decides

226
00:14:10,607 --> 00:14:11,877
whose life is worth less.

227
00:14:13,897 --> 00:14:14,827
Here's our verdict.

228
00:14:15,457 --> 00:14:19,727
It was murder. They chose a boy who
could not resist and did not agree,

229
00:14:20,077 --> 00:14:23,007
and nothing about the boat made
him the one who had to die.

230
00:14:23,657 --> 00:14:27,077
The six-month sentence was mercy, not
a verdict on what they did,

231
00:14:27,097 --> 00:14:28,497
and the mercy was right.

232
00:14:28,517 --> 00:14:31,937
Our confidence is eighty-five percent.

233
00:14:31,957 --> 00:14:36,257
What would change our mind: evidence
that Parker was hours from death

234
00:14:36,277 --> 00:14:41,427
and beyond any saving, so that killing
him cost him hours, not days.

235
00:14:44,947 --> 00:14:48,637
On the court. Coleridge was
right about the Mignonette.

236
00:14:48,907 --> 00:14:52,677
But a rule that necessity can never
excuse killing is too strong,

237
00:14:52,947 --> 00:14:54,677
and the law already knows it.

238
00:14:55,107 --> 00:14:58,347
The twins case shows there are rare
situations where nobody chooses

239
00:14:58,367 --> 00:15:01,987
the victim, and refusing to act
means everyone dies.

240
00:15:02,007 --> 00:15:05,187
Our confidence is seventy percent.

241
00:15:05,207 --> 00:15:10,187
What would change our mind: evidence
that narrow exceptions like that

242
00:15:10,207 --> 00:15:14,817
one get stretched, over time, to
excuse killings like this one.

243
00:15:18,717 --> 00:15:19,887
And the bigger question.

244
00:15:20,697 --> 00:15:22,467
Is killing to survive murder?

245
00:15:23,097 --> 00:15:27,117
Our verdict: killing an innocent
person to save yourself is never

246
00:15:27,137 --> 00:15:30,467
justified. Sometimes, it
can be forgiven.

247
00:15:31,177 --> 00:15:34,577
That's the difference between saying an
act was right and saying the

248
00:15:34,597 --> 00:15:38,737
person who did it deserves mercy, and
it's exactly what England did

249
00:15:38,757 --> 00:15:44,697
in 1884. Our confidence is lower,
sixty-five percent, because there's

250
00:15:44,717 --> 00:15:46,607
a hard case we can't fully answer.

251
00:15:46,977 --> 00:15:50,777
What would change our mind: a case
where everyone agreed to a fair

252
00:15:50,797 --> 00:15:54,357
lottery in advance, and all of them
would certainly have died without

253
00:15:54,377 --> 00:15:59,217
it. That's the hardest version of this
question, and the Mignonette

254
00:15:59,237 --> 00:16:00,427
never reached it.

255
00:16:04,267 --> 00:16:05,217
That's our verdict.

256
00:16:05,787 --> 00:16:09,217
It's open, which means it can change.

257
00:16:09,867 --> 00:16:13,177
If you think we got it wrong, make
your case in the comments.

258
00:16:13,827 --> 00:16:16,657
We'll answer the strongest one
in the next episode.

259
00:16:20,217 --> 00:16:24,017
Next case: an anatomy atlas so
detailed that surgeons still reach

260
00:16:24,037 --> 00:16:28,137
for it. It was made in Vienna, partly
under the Nazis, and some of

261
00:16:28,157 --> 00:16:31,327
its paintings probably show
people the regime executed.

262
00:16:31,997 --> 00:16:33,687
Should doctors keep using it?
